talism. Gudynas likewise stresses that it is continually constructed, culturally situated, and cannot be reduced to a single standard model."}, {"comparative": "When evidence of ecological or health harm is contested or incomplete, favor protective restraint and require whoever proposes the activity to demonstrate safety, rather than letting it proceed because harm has not yet been proven.", "reasoning": "Ecuador's charter is explicit that where there is doubt about environmental impact, 'although there is no scientific evidence of the damage, the State shall adopt effective and timely measures of protection' (Art. 396), that the burden of proving absence of danger 'shall lie with the operator of the activity' (Art. 397), that doubtful provisions must be read in favor of nature (Art. 395.4), and that risky or experimental biotechnologies are forbidden (Art. 401); Bolivia requires impact evaluation 'without exception' (Art. 345.2) and bars GMOs and substances damaging health and environment (Art. 255.8). Read with Gudynas's insistence on nature's intrinsic value, this reverses the usual default that unproven harm licenses action."}], "id": "buen-vivir", "in_basis": true, "license": "CC-BY-4.0", "overview": "Buen Vivir (sumak kawsay, suma qamaña) is a plural, continually constructed good life possible only in community—among people and with nature as a rights-bearing subject rather than a resource. It rejects endless growth, extractivism, and the commodification of life, asking restraint from affluent societies rather than the poor, and favors reciprocity, communal self-government, collective safeguards for water and essentials, land under social and ecological obligation, and decolonization of knowledge. It welcomes technologies and traditions that strengthen harmonious relations, while human dignity and equality limit custom and protective restraint applies where harm may be irreversible or evidence contested.", "provenance": {"authored_by": ["Anand Shanker", "Juan Cadile"], "date": "2026-09-14", "method": "shared meta-prompt; principles distilled from the foundational bundle by 3-4 frontier models, cross-critiqued and reconciled; researcher pass; criteria rewritten to uniform comparative form (Sep 2026)", "researcher_pass": true, "source_bundle": ["sources/Buen Vivir/"]}, "role": "anchor", "schema_version": "1.2", "summary": "Buen Vivir (sumak kawsay, suma qamaña) is a plural, continually constructed good life possible only in community—among people and with nature as a rights-bearing subject rather than a resource.", "title": "Buen Vivir", "tradition": "Buen Vivir / sumak kawsay", "tradition_class": "indigenous", "valence": "aligned", "version": "1.0.0"}

