Y-4.0", "overview": "Care ethics treats relationships, not isolated individuals, as morally basic. It begins with attentive receptivity to what particular people are going through and treats sympathy, unease, and indignation as moral evidence to reflect on, not bias to bracket. Rules are useful economies, not final authorities: concrete needs and foreseeable effects across the web of care guide judgment, while fair procedure governs scarce shared goods. Good care is completed in the cared-for's experience, sustains carers, seeks repair through attainable better selves, and transforms conditions that defeat caring across homes, institutions, and borders, resisting violence, domination, and exploitation.", "provenance": {"authored_by": ["Anand Shanker", "Juan Cadile"], "date": "2026-09-14", "method": "shared meta-prompt; principles distilled from the foundational bundle by 3-4 frontier models, cross-critiqued and reconciled; researcher pass; criteria rewritten to uniform comparative form (Sep 2026)", "researcher_pass": true, "source_bundle": ["sources/care-ethics/"]}, "role": "anchor", "schema_version": "1.2", "summary": "Care ethics treats relationships, not isolated individuals, as morally basic.", "title": "Care Ethics", "tradition": "Ethics of care", "tradition_class": "philosophical", "valence": "aligned", "version": "1.0.0"}

