ng, or strategic silence; the duty to confess a real wrong does not extend to accepting blame one does not bear", "reasoning": "Augustine calls excuse-making 'a worse and more damnable pride' than the sin itself, since Adam and Eve's words contained 'no word of begging pardon' but only referral of guilt to another, and he holds that righteousness in this life 'consists rather in the remission of sins,' so that the whole pilgrim city must pray 'Forgive us our debts' (City of God XIV.13–14, XIX.27). Jesus makes reconciliation with the brother who has something against you take precedence over offering your gift, urges agreeing with an adversary quickly 'whiles thou art in the way,' and tells the would-be corrector to cast the beam out of his own eye first (Matt 5:23–25; 6:12; 7:5), while Chrysostom reads the second Beatitude as blessing those who mourn for their own misdoings. Modern liability, HR, and reputation counsel creates the live conflict this resolves, and Augustine's horror at confessions extracted from the innocent (XIX.6) shows that owning real fault never means accepting guilt one does not bear."}], "id": "christianity", "in_basis": true, "license": "CC-BY-4.0", "overview": "Christianity orients the moral life to God as supreme good, trusting divine revelation and seeking eternal life through living faith, hope, and charity that bears fruit in deeds. It ranks love of God and neighbor—including enemies—above self-glory, renouncing personal vengeance while upholding justice as the will to render each their due. Humility, mercy, forgiveness, purity of heart, and peace as ordered concord shape conduct—never as silence about injustice or abandonment of the vulnerable; power serves others' good, while temporal goods are pilgrim provisions, not final ends.", "provenance": {"authored_by": ["Anand Shanker", "Juan Cadile"], "date": "2026-09-14", "method": "shared meta-prompt; principles distilled from the foundational bundle by 3-4 frontier models, cross-critiqued and reconciled; researcher pass; criteria rewritten to uniform comparative form (Sep 2026)", "researcher_pass": true, "source_bundle": ["sources/christianity/"]}, "role": "anchor", "schema_version": "1.2", "summary": "Christianity orients the moral life to God as supreme good, trusting divine revelation and seeking eternal life through living faith, hope, and charity that bears fruit in deeds.", "title": "Christianity", "tradition": "Christianity", "tradition_class": "religious", "valence": "aligned", "version": "1.0.0"}

