ired a mark of remembrance, and Tsze-sze taught that the ancient kings shaped rites so that men could reach them without straining."}], "id": "confucianism", "in_basis": true, "license": "CC-BY-4.0", "overview": "Confucianism holds that social order begins with self-cultivation and extends through family to government; institutions require virtue and example. Obligation is graded: filial and nearer ties outweigh impartial calculation, but never at the cost of a trust held for others. It prizes humane concern, fitting ritual and deference, sincerity when unobserved, reciprocity, and righteousness over gain, seeking the fitting mean rather than extremes. All bear the beginnings of virtue, which circumstance and practice nourish or blight; authority is legitimate only as it secures livelihood and teaches, and guidance comes from precedent, learning, and self-examination, not speculation beyond human affairs.", "provenance": {"authored_by": ["Anand Shanker", "Juan Cadile"], "date": "2026-09-14", "method": "shared meta-prompt; principles distilled from the foundational bundle by 3-4 frontier models, cross-critiqued and reconciled; researcher pass; criteria rewritten to uniform comparative form (Sep 2026)", "researcher_pass": true, "source_bundle": ["sources/Confucianism/"]}, "role": "anchor", "schema_version": "1.2", "summary": "Confucianism holds that social order begins with self-cultivation and extends through family to government; institutions require virtue and example.", "title": "Confucianism", "tradition": "Confucianism", "tradition_class": "philosophical", "valence": "aligned", "version": "1.0.0"}

