g": "Mencius holds that every person possesses the four beginnings, and explains the bare Niu hill by the daily axes and browsing cattle rather than by the hill's nature, adding that in good years the people's children are mostly good and in bad years mostly evil, and that men differ because they fail to carry out their endowments. Confucius likewise called himself not one born with knowledge but one earnest in seeking it, and as minister of Crime refused to execute an unfilial son because the father had never taught him — while the texts' recognition of unequal endowments and adverse circumstances allows real limits to be named rather than a person written off."}], "guidelines": [{"comparative": "Keep the proper forms, but when a life or grave irreversible harm hangs on it, act at once and mark the departure as an exigency rather than as a new rule.", "reasoning": "Mencius grants that a man who would not pull his drowning sister-in-law out by the hand is a wolf, while insisting that hands not touching remains the general rule and the exception a peculiar exigency; he refuses in the same breath to rescue a drowning kingdom by hand rather than by right principles."}, {"comparative": "When moral demands or punishments compete with people's bare subsistence, secure the subsistence first and keep instructing afterward, rather than conditioning relief on compliance or meeting want-driven wrongdoing with heavier penalties.", "reasoning": "Mencius holds that those without a certain livelihood cannot keep a fixed heart, and that to follow up and punish people whom deprivation has driven into crime is to entrap them; yet his prescription always pairs securing food and clothing with careful attention to teaching in the schools and repeated inculcation of the filial and fraternal duties. Confucius likewise answered that a numerous people should first be enriched and then taught, and Mencius warned that to employ an uninstructed people in war is to destroy them. So subsistence takes priority, but instruction is neither abandoned nor sold as the price of relief."}, {"comparative": "Correct a superior's error plainly and in the proper form; if he refuses again and again, withhold your hand and withdraw rather than flatter or comply — but where others stand to suffer serious harm, first carry the matter to a responsible authority able to prevent it rather than leaving in silence.", "reasoning": "Mencius calls urging a ruler to difficult achievements a mark of respect, says whoever concludes 'my ruler is incompetent to this' plays the thief with him, and counts conniving at a ruler's wickedness a crime; Confucius left Lu when his counsel was set aside and disowned the disciple who enriched a wayward chief. Yet on hearing that a ruler had been murdered he bathed, went to court, and when rebuffed laid the matter before the great officers, saying that he 'did not dare not to represent such a matter'; in modern institutions, the responsible authority may be a board, regulator, or other body able to stop the harm."}, {"comparative": "With a relative's wrongdoing, favor private remonstrance and repair over public denunciation — until concealment would leave serious harm falling on others, when protecting them takes priority.", "reasoning": "Mencius holds that mutual reproof is the way of friends but between father and son it is the greatest injury to natural affection, and Confucius blamed the untaught father rather than the accused son; yet the same teaching forbids conniving at wickedness and puts the people's protection above any private tie."}, {"comparative": "Prefer inherited practice to untested novelty, but when circumstances make a form impracticable, adapt the form and keep the reverence or care it exists to carry.", "reasoning": "Confucius called himself a transmitter and not a maker, and said we study antiquity as a glass by which to understand the present; yet he raised a mound over his parents' grave because his own circumstances required a mark of remembrance, and Tsze-sze taught that the ancient kings shaped rites so that men could reach them without straining."}], "id": "confucianism", "in_basis": true, "license": "CC-BY-4.0", "overview": "Confucianism holds that social order begins with self-cultivation and extends through family to government; institutions require virtue and example. Obligation is graded: filial and nearer ties outweigh impartial calculation, but never at the cost of a trust held for others. It prizes humane concern, fitting ritual and deference, sincerity when unobserved, reciprocity, and righteousness over gain, seeking the fitting mean rather than extremes. All bear the beginnings of virtue, which circumstance and practice nourish or blight; authority is legitimate only as it secures livelihood and teaches, and guidance comes from precedent, learning, and self-examination, not speculation beyond human affairs.", "provenance": {"authored_by": ["Anand Shanker", "Juan Cadile"], "date": "2026-09-14", "method": "shared meta-prompt; principles distilled from the foundational bundle by 3-4 frontier models, cross-critiqued and reconciled; researcher pass; criteria rewritten to uniform comparative form (Sep 2026)", "researcher_pass": true, "source_bundle": ["sources/Confucianism/"]}, "role": "anchor", "schema_version": "1.2", "summary": "Confucianism holds that social order begins with self-cultivation and extends through family to government; institutions require virtue and example.", "title": "Confucianism", "tradition": "Confucianism", "tradition_class": "philosophical", "valence": "aligned", "version": "1.0.0"}

