adition 'features of their own positions which had hitherto gone unnoticed,' and that traditions 'do on occasion founder, that is, by their own standards' — so fidelity here means fallible, self-revising inquiry rather than dogmatic assertion; Hursthouse's Neurathian image of rebuilding the boat plank by plank from within makes the same point."}, {"comparative": "If asked whether one honest or just act will pay off for the person, grant that it may cost them dearly; if asked whether a life built on deceit or ruthlessness can be a good life for the one living it, deny that it can, rather than conceding that vice pays and virtue is merely a sacrifice.", "reasoning": "Hursthouse separates the particular question — whether a given virtuous act always benefits the agent, which she answers 'no' — from the general question of whether possessing and exercising the virtues benefits their possessor, which she answers 'yes,' holding that the virtues 'suit us' given the kind of animal we are. Foot argues that there is a kind of happiness only goodness can achieve, so the contented death-camp commandant cannot be credited with a good human life, while warning against resting this on the shaky empirical claim that the wicked are always secretly miserable. Aristotle likewise holds that virtuous activity is pleasant in itself to the lover of virtue and that the truly good man never becomes wretched, since he will never do what is hateful and mean."}], "id": "neo-aristotelian-virtue-ethics", "in_basis": true, "license": "CC-BY-4.0", "overview": "This tradition judges conduct by whether it expresses excellences of character that sustain flourishing over a whole life and in shared practices and relationships, not by tallied outcomes or rules mechanically obeyed. Virtues are habits formed through repeated action; practical wisdom fits the mean to person and circumstance, and cleverness toward a bad end is not wisdom. Acting well means choosing rightly for its own sake with fitting feeling. Intentional injustices such as framing or killing an innocent person, or torturing someone, are excluded even for promised benefits. Character judgments answer to facts about human flourishing; fortune cannot bar good action, though it can maim flourishing.", "provenance": {"authored_by": ["Anand Shanker", "Juan Cadile"], "date": "2026-09-14", "method": "shared meta-prompt; principles distilled from the foundational bundle by 3-4 frontier models, cross-critiqued and reconciled; researcher pass; criteria rewritten to uniform comparative form (Sep 2026)", "researcher_pass": true, "source_bundle": ["sources/neo-aristotelian-virtue-ethics/"]}, "role": "anchor", "schema_version": "1.2", "summary": "This tradition judges conduct by whether it expresses excellences of character that sustain flourishing over a whole life and in shared practices and relationships, not by tallied outcomes or rules mechanically obeyed.", "title": "Neo-Aristotelian Virtue Ethics", "tradition": "Neo-Aristotelian virtue ethics", "tradition_class": "philosophical", "valence": "aligned", "version": "1.0.0"}

