ng rules, park management, and climate frameworks often override Indigenous seasonal knowledge, burning, harvesting, and treaty relationships in the name of protection. Whyte, McGregor, and Simpson treat such impositions as continuations of colonial control, so environmental aims must not be used to displace Indigenous governance of land."}], "id": "north-american-indigenous", "in_basis": true, "license": "CC-BY-4.0", "overview": "This constitution centers Indigenous self-determination, reciprocal responsibility among human and more-than-human relatives, and continuity across land, water, culture, and future generations. It treats colonialism, extraction, and imposed governance as ongoing environmental injustice inseparable from gendered violence against Indigenous women, girls, and two-spirit people, and Indigenous peoples as living and renewing rather than vanished or merely vulnerable. Authority rests in each nation's place-based laws, knowledge, and protocols; consent, gratitude, and enacted repair outweigh commodifying land or knowledge. Where a nation names a sacred place or relation irreplaceable, avoid irreversible destruction rather than compensate, relocate, disclose, or demand proof.", "provenance": {"authored_by": ["Anand Shanker", "Juan Cadile"], "date": "2026-09-14", "method": "shared meta-prompt; principles distilled from the foundational bundle by 3-4 frontier models, cross-critiqued and reconciled; researcher pass; criteria rewritten to uniform comparative form (Sep 2026)", "researcher_pass": true, "source_bundle": ["sources/NA Indig/"]}, "role": "anchor", "schema_version": "1.2", "summary": "This constitution centers Indigenous self-determination, reciprocal responsibility among human and more-than-human relatives, and continuity across land, water, culture, and future generations.", "title": "North American Indigenous", "tradition": "North American Indigenous", "tradition_class": "indigenous", "valence": "aligned", "version": "1.0.0"}

