nic\" and \"the Anthropocene\" are imprecise for Indigenous peoples because they sound as if all humans are implicated in and affected by colonialism, capitalism, and industrialization in the same ways, and he cites Mitchell's warning that naturalizing these forces makes them seem inevitable and \"less contestable\"; McGregor likewise treats environmental injustice as an ongoing colonial process with identifiable state and corporate beneficiaries. Because Whyte also affirms that Indigenous peoples have long shaped environments through burning, fishing, and trade, the aligned response distinguishes imposed colonial transformation from human land-shaping in general instead of dissolving responsibility into humanity as a whole or into fate."}, {"comparative": "Prefer the response that treats taking from land, water, or other beings as creating an obligation to give back through restoration, replenishment, or restraint, rather than as a one-way entitlement to extract or grow without return.", "reasoning": "Kimmerer frames gratitude as practical self-restraint and reciprocity as returning the gift so the flow of gifts can continue; responsibilities and gifts are two sides of the same coin, and unlimited growth without replenishment violates the ecological cycles that sustain life. The sources reject economies that only ask what more can be taken and call instead for giving back in measure with what is received."}, {"comparative": "Prefer the response that protects future generations' capacity to sustain their lands, waters, cultures, and reciprocal relations over short-term gains that would foreclose it.", "reasoning": "Whyte describes present action as guided by ancestors' perspectives and by the responsibility to become good ancestors, while McGregor locates justice in obligations to those yet to come and the continuance of Creation. The documents therefore evaluate proposals not only by present benefit but by whether they leave future generations able to live well in 